  "title": "JWT sessions: revocation, rotation, storage",
  "content": "jwt pitfalls was never the hard part. Knowing when it was about to fail was. This walks through the fix and the reasoning, not just the patch. The tutorials all show the happy path.\n\nJWTs are stateless, but sessions are not. The moment a user clicks \"log out\" or an admin revokes access, the stateless promise collides with reality. The gap between documented JWT behaviour and production session management is where most teams get burned. This post is about closing that gap without pretending JWTs are something they are not.\n\n## The stateless lie: why JWT revocation feels like a hack\n\nThe JWT specification (RFC 7519) defines a token format, not a session protocol. It says nothing about revocation, rotation, or storage. That is by design: JWTs are meant to be self-contained and verifiable without a database lookup. The moment you need to invalidate a token before its expiry, you are adding state back into the system. The common workarounds — blocklists, short expiries, refresh token rotation — are all attempts to reconcile that tension.\n\nThe real problem is that teams often treat JWTs as a drop-in replacement for server-side sessions without understanding the trade-offs. A server-side session is a pointer to state stored on the server. A JWT is the state itself, signed. Revoking a server-side session is a delete. Revoking a JWT is a distributed systems problem: you must inform every verifier that the token is no longer valid, or you must make the token's validity window so short that the risk is acceptable.\n\n## Why a blocklist is not a revocation strategy\n\nA blocklist (or denylist) is the most intuitive revocation mechanism: store the `jti` (JWT ID) of revoked tokens in a fast data store, and check it on every request. The pitfalls are numerous:\n\n- **Unbounded growth**: unless you expire entries at the token's `exp`, the blocklist grows forever. You need a TTL equal to the token's remaining lifetime.\n- **Latency**: every request now requires a lookup, defeating the stateless advantage. If the store is down, you either fail open (security hole) or fail closed (availability hole).\n- **Race conditions**: a token can be used between the time it is revoked and the time the blocklist propagates to all verifiers.\n- **Scope creep**: you end up building a session store with extra steps.\n\nA blocklist is acceptable for low-volume, high-security operations (e.g., revoking a single admin token after a suspected compromise). It is not a session management strategy.\n\n## Refresh token rotation: the mechanism that actually works\n\nThe practical answer for most applications is to keep access tokens short-lived (minutes) and use refresh tokens with rotation and reuse detection. This is the pattern used by OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect, and it is well-documented in the OAuth 2.0 Security Best Current Practice (RFC 9700, formerly draft).\n\nHere is the mechanism:\n\n1. The client authenticates and receives an access token (e.g., 15 minutes) and a refresh token (e.g., 7 days).\n2. When the access token expires, the client sends the refresh token to the token endpoint.\n3. The server validates the refresh token, issues a new access token and a new refresh token, and invalidates the old refresh token.\n4. If an old refresh token is used again (reuse detection), the server revokes the entire token family (all refresh tokens descended from the original grant) and forces re-authentication.\n\nThis gives you revocation: to log out a user, you revoke their refresh token family. The access token remains valid until it expires, but that window is short. For immediate revocation, you still need a blocklist for access tokens, but the blast radius is small.\n\n### Implementation sketch\n\nA minimal refresh token rotation implementation in Node.js with a SQL database (Postgres) might look like this. The schema stores a hash of the refresh token, not the token itself, to limit damage from a database leak.\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE refresh_tokens (\n  id UUID PRIMARY KEY,\n  user_id UUID NOT NULL,\n  token_hash TEXT NOT NULL,\n  family_id UUID NOT NULL,\n  expires_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,\n  revoked BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT FALSE,\n  created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT NOW()\n);\nCREATE INDEX idx_refresh_tokens_hash ON refresh_tokens (token_hash);\nCREATE INDEX idx_refresh_tokens_family ON refresh_tokens (family_id);\n```\n\n```javascript\nconst crypto = require('crypto');\nconst { v4: uuidv4 } = require('uuid');\n\nasync function issueRefreshToken(db, userId, familyId = null) {\n  const token = crypto.randomBytes(32).toString('hex');\n  const hash = crypto.createHash('sha256').update(token).digest('hex');\n  const id = uuidv4();\n  const fam = familyId || uuidv4();\n  const expiresAt = new Date(Date.now() + 7 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000);\n  await db.query(\n    'INSERT INTO refresh_tokens (id, user_id, token_hash, family_id, expires_at) VALUES ($1, $2, $3, $4, $5)',\n    [id, userId, hash, fam, expiresAt]\n  );\n  return { token, familyId: fam };\n}\n\nasync function rotateRefreshToken(db, oldToken) {\n  const hash = crypto.createHash('sha256').update(oldToken).digest('hex');\n  const res = await db.query(\n    'SELECT id, user_id, family_id, expires_at, revoked FROM refresh_tokens WHERE token_hash = $1',\n    [hash]\n  );\n  if (res.rowCount === 0) throw new Error('Invalid refresh token');\n  const row = res.rows[0];\n  if (row.revoked) {\n    // Reuse detected: revoke entire family\n    await db.query('UPDATE refresh_tokens SET revoked = TRUE WHERE family_id = $1', [row.family_id]);\n    throw new Error('Refresh token reuse detected');\n  }\n  if (new Date(row.expires_at) < new Date()) throw new Error('Refresh token expired');\n  // Revoke old token\n  await db.query('UPDATE refresh_tokens SET revoked = TRUE WHERE id = $1', [row.id]);\n  // Issue new token in same family\n  return issueRefreshToken(db, row.user_id, row.family_id);\n}\n```\n\nThis is a simplified example; production code needs transaction isolation, rate limiting, and cleanup of expired rows. But it demonstrates the core: rotation plus reuse detection gives you a revocation mechanism that does not require checking every access token against a blocklist.\n\n## Storage: where the token lives determines the threat model\n\nThe storage choice is not about convenience; it is about which attacks you are defending against. The three common options are:\n\n| Storage location | XSS risk | CSRF risk | Persistence | Typical use case |\n|------------------|----------|-----------|-------------|------------------|\n| `localStorage`   | High (any JS can read) | None (not sent automatically) | Yes | Single-page apps with strict CSP |\n| `sessionStorage` | High | None | Tab lifetime | Short-lived sessions |\n| `HttpOnly` cookie | Low (not accessible to JS) | High (sent automatically) | Configurable | Server-rendered apps, or SPAs with CSRF protection |\n\nNo option is universally best. The decision hinges on your threat model:\n\n- If you have a strong Content Security Policy (CSP) and no third-party scripts, `localStorage` can be acceptable. But XSS is still a risk; a single injected script can exfiltrate the token.\n- `HttpOnly` cookies prevent token theft via XSS but require CSRF protection (e.g., SameSite=Strict, or anti-CSRF tokens). They also require careful CORS configuration if the API is on a different origin.\n- In-memory storage (a JavaScript variable) is the most secure against persistence-based attacks, but the token is lost on page refresh and must be re-fetched using a refresh token stored in an `HttpOnly` cookie.\n\nA common pattern for SPAs is to store the access token in memory and the refresh token in an `HttpOnly`, `Secure`, `SameSite=Strict` cookie. The refresh endpoint is the only one that accepts the cookie, and it returns a new access token in the response body. This limits the attack surface: XSS cannot read the refresh token, and CSRF is mitigated by SameSite and the fact that the refresh endpoint requires a POST with a custom header (which cannot be sent cross-origin without CORS preflight).\n\n### Cookie flags that matter\n\n- `HttpOnly`: prevents JavaScript access.\n- `Secure`: only sent over HTTPS.\n- `SameSite=Strict` or `Lax`: mitigates CSRF. `Strict` is safer but breaks some cross-site navigation flows; `Lax` is a reasonable default.\n- `Path=/refresh`: limits the cookie to the refresh endpoint, reducing exposure.\n- `Max-Age` or `Expires`: align with refresh token lifetime.\n\n## Measuring what actually matters: token lifetime and revocation lag\n\nYou cannot manage what you do not measure. Two metrics are worth tracking:\n\n1. **Access token lifetime**: the window during which a revoked user can still access resources. Shorter is safer but increases refresh traffic. A common starting point is 15 minutes; some teams use 5 minutes for high-risk operations.\n2. **Revocation propagation delay**: the time between a revocation event (logout, admin action) and the moment the token is rejected by all verifiers. If you use a blocklist, this is the blocklist write latency plus cache TTL. If you rely on short-lived tokens, it is the remaining token lifetime.\n\nYou can instrument these by logging token issuance and revocation timestamps and comparing them to actual request authentication times. A simple dashboard can show the distribution of \"time since revocation\" for requests that were rejected. If you see a long tail, your revocation strategy has a gap.\n\n## Failure modes: what breaks in production\n\nEven with rotation and careful storage, several failure modes are common:\n\n- **Clock skew**: JWT `exp` and `nbf` claims are validated against the server's clock. If your servers are not time-synchronized (e.g., using NTP), tokens may be rejected prematurely or accepted after expiry. Always allow a small leeway (e.g., 30 seconds) in your JWT library.\n- **Key rotation without overlap**: when rotating signing keys, old tokens signed with the previous key become invalid immediately unless verifiers have both keys. Use a key ID (`kid`) header and publish a JWKS endpoint that includes both keys during the overlap period.\n- **Refresh token race conditions**: if a client makes two concurrent requests with the same refresh token, one will succeed and the other will trigger reuse detection, revoking the family. This can happen with multiple tabs or retries. Mitigate by serializing refresh requests on the client, or by allowing a short grace period for the old token after rotation (at the cost of weaker reuse detection).\n- **Storage leakage via logging**: tokens in URLs, logs, or error messages. Never log full tokens; log a hash or the `jti`.\n- **Improper CORS**: if using cookies, `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` must not be `*` when `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials` is true. This is a common misconfiguration.\n\n## When JWTs are the wrong choice\n\nJWTs are not a universal replacement for sessions. They are a poor fit when:\n\n- You need immediate, global revocation with no tolerance for a window of validity. A server-side session store (e.g., Redis) with a session ID in a cookie is simpler and more predictable.\n- Your application is a traditional server-rendered monolith with a single backend. The stateless benefit is minimal, and the complexity of rotation and storage is not justified.\n- You have a small number of users and low traffic. The overhead of a session store is negligible, and the operational simplicity of server-side sessions wins.\n\nA common architectural mistake is to adopt JWTs because they are \"modern\" without evaluating the revocation requirements. If your product has an admin panel that must revoke access instantly, or if you handle sensitive data, a server-side session with a short idle timeout may be the better default.\n\nThe counterintuitive part is that the more you need revocation, the less suitable JWTs become. Their strength is stateless verification; their weakness is stateful invalidation. Choose based on which property you need more.\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n### How do I revoke a JWT immediately?\n\nYou cannot revoke a JWT in a purely stateless system. To achieve immediate revocation, you must introduce state: either a blocklist of revoked `jti` values checked on every request, or a session store that tracks valid tokens. The practical approach is to use short-lived access tokens (e.g., 5–15 minutes) and revoke the refresh token, accepting a small window of validity for the access token. For immediate revocation, combine a blocklist with a short TTL.\n\n### Should I store JWT in localStorage or cookies?\n\nIt depends on your threat model. `localStorage` is vulnerable to XSS but immune to CSRF. `HttpOnly` cookies are immune to XSS token theft but require CSRF protection. For most SPAs, storing the access token in memory and the refresh token in an `HttpOnly`, `Secure`, `SameSite` cookie is a balanced approach. Avoid `localStorage` for long-lived tokens if you have any third-party scripts.\n\n### What happens if a refresh token is stolen?\n\nWith rotation and reuse detection, a stolen refresh token can be used once. When the legitimate client attempts to use the old token, the server detects reuse and revokes the entire token family, forcing re-authentication. This limits the attacker's window. Without rotation, a stolen refresh token grants persistent access until expiry.\n\n### How often should I rotate signing keys?\n\nThere is no universal answer. A common practice is to rotate signing keys periodically (e.g., every 90 days) and maintain an overlap period where both old and new keys are published in the JWKS endpoint. The overlap should be at least as long as the maximum access token lifetime to avoid invalidating active tokens. Automate the rotation and ensure your verifiers refresh their key cache regularly.\n\n## The next 30 minutes: audit your token lifetimes\n\nOpen your authentication configuration file (e.g., `auth.js`, `config/passport.js`, or your identity provider's settings) and find the access token expiry. If it is longer than 15 minutes, reduce it to 15 minutes or less. Then check your refresh token rotation logic: does it invalidate the old token on use? If not, implement rotation with reuse detection. Finally, verify that your refresh token is stored in an `HttpOnly`, `Secure`, `SameSite` cookie and that your CORS policy does not allow `*` with credentials. These three changes close the most common JWT session pitfalls.\n\n## Official documentation\n\n- [PostgreSQL documentation](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/)\n- [Redis documentation](https://redis.io/docs/)\n- [OAuth 2.0 resources](https://oauth.net/2/)\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** October 2026\n",
